1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,329
January 15th, 1919.

2
00:00:03,329 --> 00:00:04,154
Boston.

3
00:00:04,154 --> 00:00:06,852
It's lunchtime, it's unusually warm for

4
00:00:06,852 --> 00:00:10,150
winter, and people are out enjoying it.

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,462
Then they hear a sound like a machine gun.

6
00:00:13,462 --> 00:00:14,893
The ground shakes.

7
00:00:14,893 --> 00:00:18,028
And a wall of something dark and thick, taller

8
00:00:18,028 --> 00:00:21,162
than a two-story house, comes rolling down the

9
00:00:21,162 --> 00:00:23,602
street at 35 miles an hour.

10
00:00:24,052 --> 00:00:25,277
It isn't water.

11
00:00:25,277 --> 00:00:26,443
It's molasses.

12
00:00:26,443 --> 00:00:29,704
And before the day is over, it will kill 21

13
00:00:29,704 --> 00:00:30,835
people.

14
00:00:36,785 --> 00:00:39,586
In 1919 the North End was a crowded,

15
00:00:39,586 --> 00:00:43,165
working-class neighborhood, mostly Italian and

16
00:00:43,165 --> 00:00:46,744
Irish immigrant families packed into tenements

17
00:00:46,744 --> 00:00:48,589
near the harbor.

18
00:00:49,039 --> 00:00:52,141
Right in the middle of it, on Commercial

19
00:00:52,141 --> 00:00:55,708
Street, stood a giant steel tank: 50 feet tall

20
00:00:55,708 --> 00:00:57,181
and 90 feet across.

21
00:00:57,181 --> 00:01:00,450
It belonged to the Purity Distilling Company,

22
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:03,502
part of a bigger corporation called United

23
00:01:03,502 --> 00:01:05,991
States Industrial Alcohol.

24
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:08,179
Inside was molasses.

25
00:01:08,179 --> 00:01:09,851
And not for baking.

26
00:01:09,851 --> 00:01:13,005
Molasses could be fermented into industrial

27
00:01:13,005 --> 00:01:16,013
alcohol, which was used to make munitions

28
00:01:16,013 --> 00:01:17,407
during World War I.

29
00:01:17,407 --> 00:01:20,348
That made it very profitable.

30
00:01:20,798 --> 00:01:24,548
The tank went up in 1915, and it was rushed.

31
00:01:24,548 --> 00:01:27,555
The man in charge of building it was a company

32
00:01:27,555 --> 00:01:30,235
treasurer with no engineering background.

33
00:01:30,235 --> 00:01:32,655
He couldn't even read a blueprint.

34
00:01:32,655 --> 00:01:35,587
By most accounts, the tank was never properly

35
00:01:35,587 --> 00:01:38,323
tested by filling it with water before the

36
00:01:38,323 --> 00:01:40,031
molasses went in.

37
00:01:40,481 --> 00:01:43,304
And from the start, it leaked.

38
00:01:44,054 --> 00:01:46,827
Molasses seeped through the seams so badly

39
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:49,864
that local families brought buckets to collect

40
00:01:49,864 --> 00:01:50,657
it for free.

41
00:01:50,657 --> 00:01:53,713
Neighbors said the tank groaned and rumbled.

42
00:01:53,713 --> 00:01:55,425
And the company's fix?

43
00:01:55,425 --> 00:01:58,206
They painted the tank brown, so the leaks

44
00:01:58,206 --> 00:02:00,365
would be harder to see.

45
00:02:04,715 --> 00:02:06,503
Now here's the twist.

46
00:02:06,503 --> 00:02:09,881
By January 1919, the war was over, but the

47
00:02:09,881 --> 00:02:13,419
alcohol business was facing a bigger threat:

48
00:02:13,419 --> 00:02:14,384
Prohibition.

49
00:02:14,384 --> 00:02:17,546
The amendment banning alcohol was about to be

50
00:02:17,546 --> 00:02:20,779
ratified, and producers wanted to make as much

51
00:02:20,779 --> 00:02:24,189
as they could while they still had time.

52
00:02:24,639 --> 00:02:28,313
So the tank was close to full, holding about

53
00:02:28,313 --> 00:02:30,582
2.3 million gallons.

54
00:02:31,032 --> 00:02:33,083
Then the weather got weird.

55
00:02:33,083 --> 00:02:35,924
Just a day or two earlier, Boston had been

56
00:02:35,924 --> 00:02:38,428
bitterly cold, close to zero degrees.

57
00:02:38,428 --> 00:02:41,779
On the morning of the 15th, the temperature

58
00:02:41,779 --> 00:02:43,026
shot up past 40.

59
00:02:43,026 --> 00:02:45,640
A fresh shipment of warmer molasses had

60
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:48,455
recently been pumped in on top of the cold

61
00:02:48,455 --> 00:02:50,462
stuff already inside.

62
00:02:50,912 --> 00:02:52,634
Warm molasses expands.

63
00:02:52,634 --> 00:02:53,740
It ferments.

64
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:55,278
It builds pressure.

65
00:02:55,278 --> 00:02:58,299
And all of that was pushing against steel that

66
00:02:58,299 --> 00:03:01,255
was already too thin, held together by rivets

67
00:03:01,255 --> 00:03:03,563
that were already failing.

68
00:03:07,913 --> 00:03:11,587
Around 12:30 in the afternoon, the tank gave

69
00:03:11,587 --> 00:03:12,521
way.

70
00:03:12,971 --> 00:03:16,095
Witnesses heard a deep rumble, then a rapid

71
00:03:16,095 --> 00:03:19,293
popping as rivets shot out of the steel like

72
00:03:19,293 --> 00:03:19,874
bullets.

73
00:03:19,874 --> 00:03:23,586
The walls split open, and roughly 13,000 tons

74
00:03:23,586 --> 00:03:26,907
of molasses came out all at once.

75
00:03:27,357 --> 00:03:30,482
Molasses is about 40 percent heavier than

76
00:03:30,482 --> 00:03:30,940
water.

77
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:32,762
So this wasn't a spill.

78
00:03:32,762 --> 00:03:34,019
It was a wave.

79
00:03:34,019 --> 00:03:37,173
Estimates vary, but many accounts put it

80
00:03:37,173 --> 00:03:40,722
around 25 feet high at first, moving at about

81
00:03:40,722 --> 00:03:42,663
35 miles an hour.

82
00:03:43,113 --> 00:03:44,921
It flattened buildings.

83
00:03:44,921 --> 00:03:48,155
It ripped part of the elevated railway off its

84
00:03:48,155 --> 00:03:48,788
supports.

85
00:03:48,788 --> 00:03:51,781
It knocked a firehouse off its foundation.

86
00:03:51,781 --> 00:03:54,902
Wagons, horses and people were swept away or

87
00:03:54,902 --> 00:03:55,398
buried.

88
00:03:55,398 --> 00:03:58,476
At the crushed firehouse, rescuers spent hours

89
00:03:58,476 --> 00:04:01,419
cutting through the floorboards to reach the

90
00:04:01,419 --> 00:04:03,357
men trapped beneath.

91
00:04:03,807 --> 00:04:07,132
And that was just the first few seconds.

92
00:04:07,582 --> 00:04:10,839
Because the molasses was about to do something

93
00:04:10,839 --> 00:04:12,218
even worse.

94
00:04:17,368 --> 00:04:20,772
When molasses flows fast, it acts almost like

95
00:04:20,772 --> 00:04:21,452
a liquid.

96
00:04:21,452 --> 00:04:24,809
But once that wave spread out and hit the cold

97
00:04:24,809 --> 00:04:27,143
January air, it started to cool.

98
00:04:27,143 --> 00:04:29,953
And cold molasses thickens.

99
00:04:30,403 --> 00:04:33,619
People who survived the impact were now stuck

100
00:04:33,619 --> 00:04:35,764
in it, like flies on flypaper.

101
00:04:35,764 --> 00:04:39,060
The more they struggled, the more tired they

102
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:39,360
got.

103
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:42,510
Rescuers wading in got stuck too.

104
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:46,051
Sailors from a nearby training ship were among

105
00:04:46,051 --> 00:04:48,739
the first to rush in, along with police,

106
00:04:48,739 --> 00:04:50,822
firefighters and the Red Cross.

107
00:04:50,822 --> 00:04:53,982
They pulled people out of a brown, sticky mess

108
00:04:53,982 --> 00:04:56,043
that was waist-deep in places.

109
00:04:56,043 --> 00:04:58,845
Rescue work went on for days.

110
00:04:59,295 --> 00:05:02,451
21 people died, some of them children.

111
00:05:02,451 --> 00:05:05,244
Around 150 were injured.

112
00:05:05,244 --> 00:05:07,972
One victim wasn't found for nearly four

113
00:05:07,972 --> 00:05:09,062
months.

114
00:05:13,812 --> 00:05:16,127
Cleaning it up was a nightmare.

115
00:05:16,127 --> 00:05:18,875
Crews eventually used salt water from the

116
00:05:18,875 --> 00:05:21,690
harbor to break the molasses down, because

117
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:24,300
fresh water barely touched it.

118
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:27,605
The mess got tracked everywhere: onto

119
00:05:27,605 --> 00:05:30,691
streetcars, into homes, across the city.

120
00:05:30,691 --> 00:05:34,969
Boston Harbor reportedly ran brown for months.

121
00:05:35,419 --> 00:05:38,223
And people who lived in the North End for

122
00:05:38,223 --> 00:05:41,096
decades afterward swore that on hot summer

123
00:05:41,096 --> 00:05:44,226
days, you could still smell molasses.

124
00:05:48,577 --> 00:05:51,261
So who was responsible?

125
00:05:52,011 --> 00:05:55,180
The company had an answer ready: anarchists.

126
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:58,159
They claimed someone had bombed the tank.

127
00:05:58,159 --> 00:06:01,578
And in 1919, during a wave of real anarchist

128
00:06:01,578 --> 00:06:04,996
bombings, that wasn't an impossible story to

129
00:06:04,996 --> 00:06:05,985
sell.

130
00:06:06,435 --> 00:06:09,164
But the victims weren't buying it.

131
00:06:09,164 --> 00:06:12,634
119 plaintiffs, including families of the

132
00:06:12,634 --> 00:06:16,358
dead, banded together and sued United States

133
00:06:16,358 --> 00:06:17,966
Industrial Alcohol.

134
00:06:17,966 --> 00:06:20,335
It became one of the first major

135
00:06:20,335 --> 00:06:23,370
class-action-style lawsuits against a big

136
00:06:23,370 --> 00:06:25,525
American corporation.

137
00:06:25,975 --> 00:06:28,401
The case dragged on for years.

138
00:06:28,401 --> 00:06:31,665
A court-appointed auditor heard testimony from

139
00:06:31,665 --> 00:06:34,575
thousands of witnesses and a long list of

140
00:06:34,575 --> 00:06:37,626
experts, including engineers and scientists

141
00:06:37,626 --> 00:06:41,064
arguing over metal, pressure and rivets.

142
00:06:41,514 --> 00:06:44,681
In the end, the ruling was clear: there was no

143
00:06:44,681 --> 00:06:45,025
bomb.

144
00:06:45,025 --> 00:06:48,059
The tank was simply too weak for what it held,

145
00:06:48,059 --> 00:06:50,770
and the company had cut corners.

146
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:55,547
The company was ordered to pay about $628,000

147
00:06:55,547 --> 00:06:59,393
in damages, which is millions in today's

148
00:06:59,393 --> 00:07:00,570
money.

149
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,978
Here's why this story still matters, and why

150
00:07:07,978 --> 00:07:11,705
you probably benefit from it without knowing.

151
00:07:12,155 --> 00:07:15,449
After the flood, Massachusetts and then other

152
00:07:15,449 --> 00:07:18,451
states began requiring that engineers and

153
00:07:18,451 --> 00:07:21,746
architects check their calculations, sign off

154
00:07:21,746 --> 00:07:24,968
on their plans, and have projects inspected.

155
00:07:24,968 --> 00:07:28,340
Rules we now take for granted in construction

156
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:31,638
trace back, in part, to a tank of syrup that

157
00:07:31,638 --> 00:07:33,437
burst in Boston.

158
00:07:33,887 --> 00:07:37,091
Today the spot where the tank stood is a park

159
00:07:37,091 --> 00:07:38,302
with ball fields.

160
00:07:38,302 --> 00:07:41,152
The only sign of what happened is a small

161
00:07:41,152 --> 00:07:44,533
plaque that most people walk right past.

162
00:07:46,283 --> 00:07:49,754
21 lives lost, all because a company painted

163
00:07:49,754 --> 00:07:53,983
over its warning signs instead of fixing them.

164
00:07:54,433 --> 00:07:55,975
A wave of molasses.

165
00:07:55,975 --> 00:07:58,256
A cover-up story about bombers.

166
00:07:58,256 --> 00:08:01,281
And a lawsuit that helped make the buildings

167
00:08:01,281 --> 00:08:03,049
around you safer.

168
00:08:03,999 --> 00:08:05,423
No way.

169
00:08:06,573 --> 00:08:09,753
If you want more true stories that sound made

170
00:08:09,753 --> 00:08:10,742
up, subscribe.

171
00:08:10,742 --> 00:08:13,768
And if you liked this one, watch another one

172
00:08:13,768 --> 00:08:16,176
next, because they're just as wild.

